Add the Ask Flux pill and the panel it opens.

A conversation about Flux, beside the page. The button goes in the titlebar
band next to the avatar rather than in the toolbar row: the band is already
laid out by FluxBrowserViewLayout, so it costs one slot in code this fork
owns, where the toolbar would mean patching toolbar_view.cc - a file Chromium
rewrites constantly - for a few pixels of position.

The panel is laid out against the contents area, not the window. That is the
difference between it and the sidebar: the sidebar is a column of chrome and
the tab strip starts beside it, while this sits over the page with the tab
strip and toolbar running full width above. FluxBrowserViewLayout does it by
shrinking the contents bounds the base already computed, because the base is
what knows where the contents area ends up after the toolbar, the bookmarks
bar and any side panel have had their say.

Both are built for every normal window. The panel starts closed and
flux.ask_panel_open decides whether the layout reserves room for it, so the
pill and the panel's own close button are two controls over one piece of
state.

